WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.560
Cross a fruit fly with two mutations to a fly with only recessive alleles.

2
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.960
Mendel's law of independent assortment predicts that 500 of a thousand offspring

3
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.300
look like one grandparent or the other.

4
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.420
You count 830. Something is holding those alleles together.

5
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.560
Genes that lie near each other on one chromosome are called linked.

6
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:36.033
Take two loci, A and B, each a gene's place on a chromosome.

7
00:00:36.913 --> 00:00:38.393
Our fly is a female.

8
00:00:39.013 --> 00:00:44.833
She got dominant A and B on one chromosome, and recessive a and

9
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:45.993
b on the other.

10
00:00:46.753 --> 00:00:49.673
The tester gives only a and b.

11
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:55.253
So each offspring shows which alleles came in her gamete, and counting offspring

12
00:00:55.253 --> 00:00:56.493
counts her gametes.

13
00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:03.707
Each chromosome has copied itself into two sister chromatids, and the partners pair

14
00:01:03.707 --> 00:01:11.027
side by side. Here, one crossover falls between the two loci and swaps matching

15
00:01:11.027 --> 00:01:13.887
segments of two non-sister chromatids.

16
00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:18.667
Those two chromatids now carry new combinations of alleles.

17
00:01:19.247 --> 00:01:24.327
So one crossover gives two recombinant products and two parental ones.

18
00:01:24.327 --> 00:01:30.407
The farther apart two loci lie, the more often a crossover falls between them.

19
00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:37.870
In our cross, the two parental classes hold 420 and 410 flies.

20
00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.150
That is 830 parental flies.

21
00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:45.710
The two recombinant classes hold 85 flies each.

22
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:52.270
So 170 of a thousand flies are recombinant, which is 17%.

23
00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:59.690
One percent recombinant offspring is one centimorgan, so these loci are 17 centimorgans apart.

24
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.650
Recombination frequency never passes 50%.

25
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:10.730
One crossover involves only two of the four chromatids, so it gives half recombinant

26
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:16.270
products. A second crossover can put the loci back, so long intervals are

27
00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:21.750
underestimated. Haldane's function corrects for this, assuming that crossovers form

28
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.170
independently of one another.

29
00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.210
It turns our 17% into about 20.8 centimorgans.

30
00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:36.563
This female received A with b on one chromosome, and a with B

31
00:02:36.563 --> 00:02:41.063
on the other. She still gives 17% recombinants.

32
00:02:41.463 --> 00:02:47.843
But now they are A B and a b, 8.5% each.

33
00:02:49.183 --> 00:02:55.083
A b and a B are parental, 41.5% each.

34
00:02:55.083 --> 00:03:02.283
The parents define the classes, not the counts, so read them first, then map with short intervals.
